  "nl_text": "The schemabench_0215 database contains seven tables that work together to model a fictional airline company. The tables are:\n1. PASSENGER: This table stores information about the passengers, including their account ID, name, age, and frequent flyer ID. The account ID is the primary key and is not null.\n2. AIRPORT: This table stores information about the airports, including their code, name, city, state, address, classification, and time zone. The airport code is the primary key and is not null.\n3. AIRCRAFT: This table stores information about the aircraft, including their number, make, weight limit, flight number, and airport code. The airplane number is the primary key and is not null.\n4. FLIGHTCREW: This table stores information about the flight crew, including their employee ID, name, title, age, street address, zip code, city, state, and airport code. The employee ID is the primary key and is not null.\n5. FLIGHT: This table stores information about the flights, including their flight number, departure time, arrival time, flight price, airport code, gate number, airplane number, and employee ID. The flight number is the primary key and is not null.\n6. TICKET: This table stores information about the tickets, including the ticket ID, from location, to location, seat, price, flight status, class, account ID, and flight number. The ticket ID is the primary key and is not null.\n7. LUGGAGE: This table stores information about the luggage, including the luggage ID, carousel number, weight, account ID, and airplane number. The luggage ID is the primary key and is not null.\nThe tables are related to each other through foreign keys, which enforce referential integrity. For example, the FLIGHTCREW table has a foreign key to the AIRPORT table, which ensures that the airport code in the FLIGHTCREW table is valid. Similarly, the FLIGHT table has foreign keys to the AIRCRAFT and FLIGHTCREW tables, which ensure that the airplane number and employee ID in the FLIGHT table are valid.\nThe tables also have primary keys, which ensure that each row in the table is unique. The primary keys are used to identify the rows in the table and to enforce entity integrity.\nIn summary, the schemabench_0215 database schema models a fictional airline company, with tables for passengers, airports, aircraft, flight crew, flights, tickets, and luggage. The tables are related to each other through foreign keys, which enforce referential integrity, and have primary keys, which ensure that each row in the table is unique.",
  "gold_question": "American Airlines Operations Application\n\n1) Background / Context\nAmerican Airlines requires a comprehensive application to support their day-to-day operations. The system is intended to manage critical data related to passengers, airport activities, aircraft, flight crew, and flight logistics using a MySQL database.\n\n2) Objectives\n- To streamline management of flight operations, crew, and ticketing.\n- To enhance data accessibility and management for operational efficiency.\n- To integrate diverse operational components within a unified system.\n\n3) Stakeholders & Users\n- Airline Operations Managers\n- Flight Crew Members\n- Ticketing Agents\n- Passengers\n\n4) Functional Descriptions\n- Manage passenger information, including account details and frequent flyer data.\n- Maintain airport details, including codes, names, and locations.\n- Track aircraft details such as make, weight limits, and assigned flight numbers.\n- Handle flight crew details including personal information and assigned airport.\n- Schedule and manage flight information including departure, arrival times, and pricing.\n- Issue tickets with route specifics, seating, pricing, and flight status.\n- Transport luggage management, correlating with passenger and flight details.\n\n5) Non-Functional Descriptions\n- Ensure data integrity and referential integrity across all entities.\n- Support efficient data retrieval to handle operational demands.\n- Maintain system security for sensitive data related to flights and passengers.\n- Achieve high availability and reliability to support continuous airline operations.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_codellama13b/0215.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0215\nCREATE TABLE `passenger` (\n  `account_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `age` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `frequent_flyer_id` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`account_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `airport` (\n  `code` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `city` varchar(100) NOT NULL,\n  `state` varchar(100) NOT NULL,\n  `address` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `classification` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `time_zone` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`code`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `aircraft` (\n  `airplane_number` varchar(20) NOT NULL,\n  `make` varchar(100) NOT NULL,\n  `weight_limit` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `flight_number` varchar(20) NOT NULL,\n  `airport_code` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`airplane_number`),\n  CONSTRAINT `aircraft_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`airport_code`) REFERENCES `airport` (`code`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `flightcrew` (\n  `employee_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `title` varchar(100) NOT NULL,\n  `age` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `street_address` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `zip_code` varchar(10) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `city` varchar(100) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `state` varchar(100) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `airport_code` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`employee_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `flightcrew_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`airport_code`) REFERENCES `airport` (`code`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `flight` (\n  `flight_number` varchar(20) NOT NULL,\n  `departure_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `arrival_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `flight_price` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `airport_code` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `gate_number` varchar(10) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `airplane_number` varchar(20) NOT NULL,\n  `employee_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`flight_number`),\n  CONSTRAINT `flight_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`airplane_number`) REFERENCES `aircraft` (`airplane_number`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `flight_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`employee_id`) REFERENCES `flightcrew` (`employee_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `ticket` (\n  `ticket_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `from_location` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `to_location` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `seat` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `price` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `flight_status` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `class` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `account_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `flight_number` varchar(20) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`ticket_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `ticket_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`account_id`) REFERENCES `passenger` (`account_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `ticket_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`flight_number`) REFERENCES `flight` (`flight_number`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `luggage` (\n  `luggage_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `carousel_number` varchar(10) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `weight` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `account_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `airplane_number` varchar(20) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`luggage_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `luggage_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`account_id`) REFERENCES `passenger` (`account_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `luggage_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`airplane_number`) REFERENCES `aircraft` (`airplane_number`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
